{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Seven years seven times, and they shall be to you the days of seven sabbaths of years</b>, etc. What need was there to add and say, והיו לך — 'and they shall be to you'? Do we not already know that the count of seven years seven times is forty-nine years? But it may be said, in the secret of the matter, that the expression comes in recounting this exalted commandment to explain what is present and to allude to what is future. For the first count alluded to the commandment of the Jubilee, which is forty-nine years, and the fiftieth year is holy; therefore it used of it וספרת — the language of counting. And the second count alluded to the great Jubilee, which is the end of all the days of the world, whose duration is forty-nine, for the number forty-nine — in days and in years and in thousands and in the gates of understanding (binah) that were given over to Moses — is all one matter, alluding to the time of the world's standing and existence, as alluded to at the beginning of Ecclesiastes: ״והארץ לעולם עומדת״. And this is the world of the Jubilee of the world. And this is the secret of 'fifty gates of understanding (binah) were created in the world, and all of them were given over to Moses except one,' as it is said (Psalms 8:6): ״ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים״. The explanation of this is that they learned: for every thousand and thousand, one gate of understanding (binah). And He made known to him the entirety of existence from beginning to end, except for the Jubilee that is holy, which is the fiftieth, innermost gate of understanding (binah); and this is ״ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים״ — and that gate they called מעט ('a little') because of the exceeding subtlety and inwardness of it among the gates of Elohim, which is understanding (binah). Therefore he mentioned the number of a thousand years corresponding to this number, and said והיו לך — the language of existence (havayah); and he further added and said, ימי — 'days,' for all the days of the world are a number for you, and concerning this it says, והיו לך ימי — 'and they shall be to you the days of.' And to this King David, peace be upon him, alluded in saying (Psalms 145:13): ״מלכותך מלכות כל עולמים״, and the full verse is (Psalms 105:8): ״דבר צוה לאלף דור״, for a generation is like a world, and this is fifty years. And the allusion comes through the word כל ('all') and through the word לך ('to you'), for it is all one intention. And this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:16.779160118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}